      "id": "Hbol",
      "code_hash": "24a6466c8b89182c63babfbf27d63d0a",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"eddy-a-tour\">\ud83c\udf00 eddy: a tour</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><a href=\"https://ktaletsk.github.io/tidaleddy/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">eddy</a> is the pattern engine of\n<a href=\"https://tidalcycles.org\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">TidalCycles</a>, in Python. You write short patterns of\nrhythm and melody, and eddy turns them into sound, synthesized right here in the\nnotebook with numpy.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The tour starts with a few small sounds, then shows what Python adds: patterns\nbuilt by code, by a cellular automaton, by your words, and by a loop you bend\nwith the mouse.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>In every box below:</strong> press <strong>\u25b6 Play</strong>, click the code to change it (<strong>\u21b5</strong> applies),\ndrag the blue numbers, and click sound names to cycle through them. Changes come in on\nthe next loop, without stopping the music. <strong>\u25a0 Hush</strong> stops.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
